.TH SHAREDMEMPIPE 1 "November 2026" "Operating Systems I" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sharedmempipe \- transfer data between processes through shared memory, synchronized by pipes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sharedmempipe
.RB [ \-m
.IR text ]
.br
.B sharedmempipe
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sharedmempipe
creates a 4096-byte shared memory block with
.BR mmap (2)
and then calls
.BR fork (2).
The parent process (producer) copies data into the block. The child process
(consumer) writes the block to standard output.
.PP
Two pipes keep the processes in step. The producer sends the byte
.B D
on the ready pipe when the block holds new data, and waits. The consumer
sends the byte
.B A
on the acknowledgement pipe after it has written the data. Only then does the
producer reuse the block. At the end, the producer sends
.B Q
and waits for the child with
.BR waitpid (2).
.PP
Input larger than 4096 bytes is sent in several chunks. Binary data is
supported when reading from standard input.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-m " text"
Send
.I text
instead of reading standard input.
.TP
.B \-h
Print usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All data was transferred.
.TP
.B 1
A system call failed, the child failed, or the transfer was interrupted.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid options or arguments.
.SH SIGNALS
SIGINT and SIGTERM make the producer stop after the current chunk, tell the
child to quit, and exit with status 1. SIGPIPE is ignored, so a dead consumer
causes a reported error instead of a silent exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sharedmempipe \-m "hello"
printf "one\\ntwo\\n" | sharedmempipe
sharedmempipe < image.png > copy.png
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mmap (2),
.BR pipe (2),
.BR fork (2),
.BR waitpid (2),
.BR sigaction (2)
